WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.830
2018 offered a huge range of awesome, diverse
films in just about every genre… but which

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.460
are the at the top of their class?

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.500
These are the very best films 2018 has to
offer.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.300
John Krasinski, best known for his role as
Jim Halpert on The Office, probably isn't

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.020
a name that comes to mind when thinking about
the horror genre.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.460
“I ate a tuna sandwich on my first day so
Andy started calling me Big Tuna.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.440
I don’t think any of them know my real name.”

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.100
But after cutting his directorial teeth on
TV and a couple of well-received low-budget

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.220
features, Krasinski made the leap from the
world of comedy to the realm of fright flicks

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:37.060
with A Quiet Place.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.780
Krasinski stars along with his wife, Emily
Blunt, in the tale of a couple plagued by

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.420
mysterious creatures who hunt by sound, forcing
them into a tense and silent existence.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.920
Rolling Stone's Peter Travers called it an
instant horror classic, citing Blunt as a

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.270
highlight by calling her,

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.750
"... in a class by herself, taking a near-silent
role and building a tour de force of expressive

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.240
emotion."

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.580
The Toronto Sun's Liz Braun agreed, calling
A Quiet Place

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.400
"... a terrific movie, beautifully made and
carried along by strong performances and skillful

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:05.900
storytelling.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.260
It'll scare the hell out of you, too."

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.820
In a year that's shaped up to be a great one
for fans of horror, A Quiet Place stands atop

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.560
the critical heap.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.160
After ten years, Marvel Studios isn't surprising
anyone with the high quality of its films.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.470
With director Ryan Coogler and star Chadwick
Boseman onboard, Black Panther was naturally

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.260
another winner.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:28.740
The film managed to surpass even the highest
of expectations, surpassing $1.3 billion at

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.480
the worldwide box office, making it the highest-grossing
solo superhero movie of all time.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.440
The movie appeals to more diverse audiences
than standard superhero fare, and Black Panther

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.460
was simply astonishingly good.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.700
“Interesting.”

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.840
Many critics have proclaimed it to be on a
totally different level from the rest of the

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.700
MCU, exploring themes of alienation and loss
more effectively than a comic book film has

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.700
any right to.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.920
Special credit goes to Michael B. Jordan's
conflicted villain, Erik "Killmonger" Stevens,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.420
who's been cited as the MCU's best villain
ever thanks largely to the actor's towering

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:00.920
performance.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:02.060
"Don't trip.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.000
I"m gonna take this off your hands for ya.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.540
These items aren't for sale.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.060
How you think your ancestors got these?

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.620
You think they paid a fair price?"

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.900
Slashfilm summed it up the best:

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.780
"Run, don't walk, to the theater to see Black
Panther.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.170
This is not hyperbole…

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.020
Saying it's just 'good' is underselling it.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.720
[…It is perhaps] the best film of 2018."

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.940
Even the eye-popping box office success of
Black Panther was only a warm-up act for Avengers:

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.020
Infinity War, which shattered too many records
to list here.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.620
Part one of the culmination of Marvel's three-phase
mega-arc brought together nearly every hero

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.900
introduced in the MCU thus far, and fans responded
by putting up tremendous box office numbers.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:44.720
Sure, Infinity War didn't earn the widespread
critical adoration enjoyed by Black Panther,

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.540
but positive reviews recognized it as a gigantic
feat of storytelling, delivering meaningful

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.700
arcs for characters we've come to know and
love.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.640
Notably, its villain, Josh Brolin's Thanos,
moved some critics to declare the Mad Titan

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.160
as one of the MCU's most effective villains
ever.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.100
"Your planet was on the brink of collapse
I'm the one who stopped that."

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.360
The film's amazingly dark cliffhanger ending
caused some reviewers to call Infinity War

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.060
"half a movie," but perhaps their tune will
change when Avengers 4 debuts in 2019.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.460
It's no surprise here that Marvel Studios
landed their 20th straight film to debut at

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.000
No. 1 with the lively and entertaining Ant-Man
and the Wasp.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.990
Audiences needed a bit of a palate cleanser
after Thanos' big snap, and returning director

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.460
Peyton Reed gave them one.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:34.239
A worthy sequel to 2015's Ant-Man, this follow-up
is even more assured, and perhaps even funnier

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:34.980
than the original.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.140
“What’s Up!

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.860
You don’t remember that beloved commercial?

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.840
What’s up?”

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.040
Filmfare.com went so far as to call it, quote,
"the funniest Marvel film you'll see," which

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.940
is high praise considering that Thor: Ragnarok
was released less than a year prior.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.470
The film's loose, playful tone and confidently
staged action pieces make even more novel

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:07.590
use of the hero's size-changing technology
than the original, and the film's reasonably

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.390
low stakes compared to Infinity War make it
a great, light break from the MCU's recent

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.080
trend toward all-out destruction.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.659
But if you're still traumatized from Infinity
War, you may want to skip Ant-Man and the

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.820
Wasp's post-credits scene.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.720
Fans of Ryan Reynolds' Merc With a Mouth eagerly
awaited the follow-up to 2016's ridiculously

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:30.410
popular R-rated hit Deadpool, and Reynolds,
along with incoming director David Leitch

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.040
didn't disappoint.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.639
Fans of the original knew they could expect
insane action and over-the-top vulgarity,

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:39.599
but most weren't ready for the surprising
amount of character building and emotion in

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.100
the sequel.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.580
Plus, as promised, they introduced Cable in
this one.

00:04:42.969 --> 00:04:47.180
Wired gushed over the performances of the
entire cast, noting that Deadpool's assembly

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.229
of the mutant superhero team X-Force,

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:54.129
"... gives Deadpool 2 a much stronger narrative
arc, and emotional payoff, than its predecessor."

00:04:54.129 --> 00:04:56.099
They also praised the ending, calling it

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.520
"... surprisingly heartfelt…

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:02.260
This sequel actually set out to be a movie,
not just a series of jokes stuck to a Macguffin."

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.720
It's the rare sequel that might just surpass
its beloved predecessor.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.900
And some critics called its post-credits scene
the best one ever, which is really saying

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:10.400
something.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:16.240
Picking up literally seconds after the 2004
original, Incredibles 2 deals with the destructive

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.860
consequences of superhero battles, and how
the Parr family survives in a rapidly-changing

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:21.360
world.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.740
But even in this era of superhero movie box
office dominance, Incredibles 2 is unique,

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:30.620
and it still manages to stand shoulder-to-shoulder
with everything Marvel and DC have to offer.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:35.080
In one of many glowing reviews for the film,
Calvin Wilson of the St. Louis Post-Dispatch

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:35.620
called it,

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.780
"One of the most entertaining films of the
year, and one of the smartest.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.100
[...It] sets a standard that few superhero
flicks, animated or live-action, can match."

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.840
In a year typically stuffed to the brim with
high-profile superhero adventures, Incredibles

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.860
2 proves Marvel Studios aren't the only ones
who know how to score a critical hit in the

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:52.360
genre.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.800
For over two decades, Tom Cruise has put himself
in mortal danger for the surprisingly resilient

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.000
Mission: Impossible franchise, which has failed
to lose speed despite its star's advancing

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:02.500
age.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.779
Now in his 50s, Cruise continues to insist
on doing every stunt that it's physically

00:06:06.779 --> 00:06:08.349
possible for him to do.

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:11.949
Honestly, audiences don't go to a Mission:
Impossible film for character beats or high

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:12.440
drama.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.260
They come for the action, and Mission: Impossible
– Fallout delivered in spades.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:20.460
Director Christopher McQuarrie's sure hand
behind the camera, plus Cruise's dedication

00:06:20.469 --> 00:06:25.629
to the role of eternally put-upon I.M.F. agent
Ethan Hunt, resulted in a crackerjack picture

00:06:25.629 --> 00:06:28.300
which pulled down some of the best reviews
of the entire series.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.300
As impossible as it may seem, this venerable
action franchise and its star just seem to

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.639
keep getting better with age, or, as succinctly
put by the Detroit News' Adam Graham,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.980
"Bless this franchise, we're truly not worthy."

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.979
Crazy Rich Asians, featuring Constance Wu
as a native New Yorker who accompanies her

00:06:44.979 --> 00:06:49.389
boyfriend to a wedding in Singapore, became
a surprising critical smash and a cultural

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:53.900
phenomenon, as the only romantic comedy in
recent memory to feature a nearly all-Asian

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:54.400
cast.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.080
Reviewer Rendy Jones encapsulated the positive
consensus in concluding his review,

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:04.179
"Crazy Rich Asians takes familiar tropes and
revitalizes them and its entire genre [with]

00:07:04.179 --> 00:07:07.289
a great cast, amazing direction, and powerful
writing."

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:12.659
Like Black Panther before it, Crazy Rich Asians
constitutes a clear message to Hollywood execs

00:07:12.659 --> 00:07:17.550
that heartfelt, well-executed stories featuring
people of color can not only cross cultural

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:21.320
boundaries, but smash them, and earn huge
box office in the process.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:27.360
A classic Hollywood story that's been revisited
no fewer than three times since the 1937 original,

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:32.749
A Star is Born got new life in 2018 with a
lovingly crafted retelling that may prove

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:36.969
to be not only the finest version of the story
put to film, but one of the greatest remakes

00:07:36.969 --> 00:07:37.620
of all time.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.080
Directed by and starring Bradley Cooper, the
film tells the story of up-and-coming singer

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.589
Ally, played by Lady Gaga, who falls in love
with an established musician and begins her

00:07:46.589 --> 00:07:50.560
ascent to stardom even as the career of her
mentor and lover begins to decline.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.680
Cooper displays some true directorial skills;
Adam Epstein of Quartz called his direction,

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.219
quote, "instinctive [and] remarkably orchestrated",
enhancing his own spot-on performance while

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.100
taking full advantage of Gaga's smoldering
star power.

00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:07.780
To nobody's surprise, she absolutely crushes
it in her first big-screen leading role, with

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:12.259
piles of notices singling out the realistic
chemistry between the two leads and Gaga's

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.420
"powerful and winning" screen presence.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.560
Expect Oscar nominations.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.580
Striking actress Anya Taylor-Joy has been
busy carving out a niche for herself as a

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.550
modern-day scream queen, with high-visibility
roles in critically acclaimed fright flicks

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:27.100
The Witch and Split in just the last few years.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:31.860
First-time writer/director Cory Finley was
lucky enough to secure her for his debut feature,

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:35.660
the black comedy/thriller Thoroughbreds, and
with the help of his clockwork script and

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:39.260
tension-generating direction, he was able
to score a winner on his first outing.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.060
The plot follows reuniting friends Lily and
Amanda as they scheme to murder the former's

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:44.560
stepfather.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.760
Critics agree that Finley shows the kind of
self-assuredness and confidence in his vision

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.680
that normally eludes freshman filmmakers.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.760
Christy Lemire praised the film as

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.880
"... classically Hitchcockian in its smoldering
homicidal tendencies, yet bracingly current."

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.720
Other reviews singled out Finley's masterful
balance of humor and darkness.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:06.260
It seems that he nailed it, and also that
we might have a major new talent on our hands.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:11.380
Love, Simon was destined for praise for one
simple reason: it's the first major studio

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:15.840
Hollywood film to focus on a gay teen romance,
which is a groundbreaking endeavor in and

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:16.400
of itself.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:20.900
Fortunately, director Greg Berlanti delivered
a film that's notable for far more than just

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:21.500
that reason.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.200
It's a funny, disarmingly sweet rom-com that
some critics are comparing to the best work

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.180
of John Hughes.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:32.700
Working from a 2015 YA novel titled Simon
vs. The Homo Sapiens' Agenda, the script is

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:37.210
populated with strongly drawn characters,
all with feelings and motivations familiar

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:37.700
to anyone.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.000
Nick Robinson was singled out for praise in
the lead role, among a strong cast which included

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.940
Jennifer Garner, Josh Duhamel, and Alexandra
Shipp.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.270
Critic Sheila O'Malley called the film, quote,
"a radically inclusive act," which was a common

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:51.260
theme throughout most of the glowing reviews.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:53.580
Den of Geek's Delia Harrington proclaimed
it,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.860
"A rare, uplifting, 'romantic AF' LGBTQ movie…
to be prized.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:02.100
With a modern soundtrack and a diverse cast,
Love, Simon is the kind of movie many people

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.140
wish they had growing up."

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.820
The films in the Transformers series have
produced consistently diminishing returns

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.030
both critically and at the box office, and
you could be forgiven for expecting Bumblebee

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:13.600
to offer more of the same.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:18.070
But with director Travis Knight taking over
for Michael Bay and working from a smart,

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:23.390
'80s-set script, a funny thing happened: Bumblebee
debuted to stellar reviews, with near-universal

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.900
praise for its smaller, more personal story
and throwback vibe.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:31.800
Meanwhile, lead Hailee Steinfeld was singled
out for her heartfelt, nuanced performance,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:35.120
surely the first time those words have been
applied to a Transformers movie.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:36.920
For proof of that, see exhibit A:

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.280
"Bumblebee, stop lubricating the man."

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:51.860
Simply put, Bumblebee served up what the series
had thus far struggled to provide: heart,

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:52.780
wonder, and fun.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:55.640
"God I'm so screwed."

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.340
Sony Animation's Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse
skipped the traditional Peter Parker-focused

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.880
Spidey story in favor of fan favorite Miles
Morales.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:10.260
Based loosely on the Spider-Verse comics event,
in which multiple Spider-people from different

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.820
universes must band together to take on a
common threat, the film leaned into the idea

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.810
that "anybody could be behind the mask" — and
followed through with its wildly eclectic

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.300
collection of Spider-characters.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:21.480
"Hey guys.”

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:22.540
“Wanda?”

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.340
“It’s Gwen, actually.”

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:25.500
“Haha.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:26.100
You know her.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:27.920
Very cool."

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.920
In addition to its spot-on characterizations
and story, the flick drew tons of praise for

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:37.360
its insanely innovative animation style, rendered
to look as if it were leaping right off the

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.360
comics page.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.720
Some critics went so far as to call it the
best Spider-Man film ever made, with others

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:46.480
suggesting it should be a shoo-in for an Academy
Award in the animation category.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:51.180
An unmitigated triumph for Sony, Into the
Spider-Verse opened up whole new avenues of

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:56.720
storytelling for Spidey and garnered wider
acclaim than nearly any wide release of 2018.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.800
Check out one of our newest videos right here.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:03.260
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:06.509
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

